so a lot has happened since the last  time we checked in on the activision  blizzard scandal and with the government  suing them allegations that management  enabled sexual harassment assault and  discrimination at the company and now  accusations that hr shredded documents  detailing those allegations you'd expect  a pretty significant response  well they're gonna change mccree's name  so in case you've been living under a  rock activision blizzard is being sued  by the state of california for allegedly  fostering a workplace culture that  enabled sexual harassment and abuse as  well as for discriminating against women  when it came to equal pay and promotions  this lawsuit was quickly followed by  hundreds of current and former activist  blizzard employees sharing their own  stories online about how they  experienced or witnessed sexual  harassment and misconduct at work this  was followed by activision blizzard  hiring a law firm mostly known for  busting unions to handle their internal  review people walking out on the job in  protest and then a bunch of higher ups  at activision blizzard quietly leaving  or being let go from the company but now  we get to some of the more public facing  falling out at least for activision  blizzard on august 10th the ceredo's  michael william reported that some  overwatch league casters were not using  the name mccree to describe overwatch's  cowboy themed dps character during  league matches the character has been in  overwatch since the game launched and  was named after former world of warcraft  and diablo developer jesse mccree jesse  mccree the real person not the digital  cowboy was spotted in a screenshot of a  chat log called blizzcon cosby crew  uploaded by former world of warcraft  creative lead alexa franciabe to his  personal facebook kotaku's ethan gatch  first reported on a frosty abbey's  facebook post about the cosby suite the  cosby suite was first mentioned in the  lawsuit in which the state of california  stated that it was a frosty yabbi's  hotel room at blizzcon and it was named  as such because of frasier's reputation  as someone who allegedly sexually  harassed women in the weeks after the  lawsuit came out people who hung out at  the cosby suite over the years stated  that actually the name was a joke about  bill cosby's ugly sweaters and  apparently had nothing to do with a  frosty obby's alleged reputation or the  allegations of rape made against bill  cosby however kotaku reported that  according to unnamed sources that  reputation is why afrosiyabi and other  men were photographed posing with a  picture of cosby in the hotel room this  all implicated mccree in a culture of  workplace abuse and harassment and on  august 11th gatch reported that mccree  was let go from blizzard then on august  26th the official overwatch twitter  account released a statement saying they  were going to rename the character quote  currently known as mccree to something  that better represents what overwatch  stands for  yes  you should have never done that  it was dumb in the beginning  it was stupid  yeah it was dumb  it's stupid like it's a stupid decision  to solve another stupid decision as an  aside while i do think it's only a good  thing to change this character's name  that wording implies you're gonna name  him something absurdly performative like  what name represents opposing sexual  harassment anyway apparently this name  change isn't gonna happen for a while  the character was supposed to have a  story event in september and now that's  being delayed to include the name change  the statement also says that going  forward characters aren't going to be  named after real-life employees which is  a problem that other blizzard games are  also dealing with right now until  recently mafrasiyabi had an npc in wow  named after him and mccree has several  characters in the wow named after him on  top of an entire city kotaku reported  that two other devs left blizzard  alongside mccree former diablo for  director luis barriga and former wow  developer jonathan will craft and on  august 26th the washington post nathan  grayson reported that references in wow  to borriga and with craft are also being  removed all this was happening just days  after a bombshell report from axios's  megan for manesh and steven tottillo who  reported that the state of california  was expanding its lawsuit against  activision blizzard to add temporary  workers alongside full-time employees  and that the state was alleging  activision blizzard tried to interfere  with their investigation according to  axios california's department of fair  employment and housing or the dfeh says  that activated blizzard is trying to  block employees from talking to the fbh  through ndas and by forcing them to talk  to activision blizzard before they go to  the dfeh and on top of all of that the  biggest thing in this axios report is  that the feh is alleging that activision  blizzard hr shredded documents related  to the investigation and complaints  essentially the lawsuit now alleges that  activision blizzard is destroying  evidence axios received a statement from  activation blizzard that said with  regards to claims that we have destroyed  information by shredding documents those  claims are not true we took appropriate  steps to preserve information relevant  to the dfvh investigation so yeah  activision blizzard gets a ton of bad  press for allegedly shredding documents  related to investigation about how they  may have enabled sexual harassment abuse  and discrimination at the company and  their response is to change the cowboy's  name  cool amidst all this an eagle-eyed  twitter user named the auxon 619 noticed  that activision's logo isn't in any of  the trailers for the upcoming call of  duty vanguard as kotaku's ian walker  pointed out activision's logo has been  prominently featured in cod trailers  since at least 2011. in a statement to  kotaku an activision blizzard  spokesperson stated that call of duty  has continued to expand into an  incredible universe of experiences this  was a creative choice that reflects how  vanguard represents the next major  installment in the franchise this has  become international news and as a  result activision blizzard has come to  the conclusion that the next call of  duty game will sell worse if it's  associated with well activision it's  shameless but it should show you just  how scared activision blizzard is right  now like we talked about last time they  are under serious pressure from a state  government consequences are coming if  they haven't already begun like i said  before i think changing cowboy  mcshooters in his name is a good idea  naming things after employees is  basically always going to go wrong but  if you're willing to do it you have to  be ready to change the name if that  person ends up getting milkshake ducked  i'm sure plenty of overwatch fans are  going to feel happier and even safer  knowing that someone who allegedly  participated in that toxic workplace  culture isn't memorializing their  favorite game and that's only a good  thing but that's all it is in my opinion  changing this name is a token gesture  that doesn't actually address the issues  here like activision removing their logo  from cod trailers it's a distraction  nothing else it's a distraction from a  looming lawsuit from allegations that  top brass aren't being held accountable  from employee demands not being listened  to from accusations that hr is shredding  documents presumably in an attempt to  soften the lawsuit all i'm saying is  don't let the smoke get in your eyes  nothing has changed at least  not yet  thanks for watching if you want more  content like this hit the sub button and  ring that notification bell for unique  bite size videos you won't find anywhere  else hit up our instagram twitter and  facebook pages
 

